# Slow halide exchange in CsPbIBr2 films for high-efficiency, carbon-based, all-inorganic perovskite solar cells

ABSTRACT Halide exchange offers a versatile way to modify the properties of halide perovskites, but it is particularly challenging to slow the reaction rate to restrain defect growth in the products. Herein, we propose a slow halide exchange strategy to simultaneously fine-tune the optical and microstructural characteristics of $\mathbf{Cs}\mathbf{Pb}\mathbf{IB}\mathbf{r}_{2}$ films by physically pairing $\mathbf{CsPbIBr}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ films. Once a proper heating treatment is applied, halide exchange of $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}^{-}$ and ${\boldsymbol{\mathbf{I}}}^{-}$ ions between the films is activated, and the reaction rate can be well-controlled by the heating recipe, in which a high temperature can accelerate the exchange reaction, while a low temperature slows or stops it. By using an optimal halide exchange temperature $\mathbf{(110^{\circ}C)}$ and time $(2\mathbf{h})$ , the parent $\mathbf{CsPbIBr}_{2}$ film was transformed into high-quality $\mathbf{CsPbI_{1+x}B r}_{2-x}$ film, featuring an extended absorption onset from 590 to ${\bf625n m}$ , coarsened grains, improved crystallinity, reduced surface roughness, suppressed halide phase segregation, and identical stability to the pristine film. Accordingly, the efficiency of a carbon-based, all-inorganic perovskite solar cell (PSC) was boosted to $10.94\%$ , which was much higher than that of the pristine $\mathbf{CsPbIBr}_{2}$ film $(8.21\%)$ . The $\mathbf{CsPbI_{1+x}B r}_{2-x}$ PSC also possessed excellent tolerance against heat and moisture stresses.
Keywords: halide exchange, slow reaction rate, $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{IBr}_{2}$ , defects, all-inorganic perovskite solar cells
## INTRODUCTION
As an emerging photovoltaic technology, perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have achieved noticeable progress in the past decade [1–3]. In particular, their power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) are now comparable to silicon solar cells, with a record value of $25.4\%$ [4]. However, the inherently poor operational stability of conventional PSCs with a typical configuration of fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO)/ electron-transport layer (ETL)/hybrid perovskite/holetransport layer (HTL)/metal electrode seems to hinder their further commercialization prospects [2,5]. This issue primarily stems from the chemical instability and decomposition tendency of hybrid perovskite films, as well as the degradation of the HTL/metal electrode interface [6,7]. Alternatively, carbon-based, all-inorganic PSCs have been proposed in which a hybrid perovskite is replaced by a more stable all-inorganic one, and the HTL and metal electrode are substituted with a cheap, inert carbon electrode prepared by well-established screenprinting technology [8–11]. The resulting carbon-based, all-inorganic PSCs are endowed with superior stability, low cost, and easy fabrication.
Among the various inorganic perovskites explored for carbon-based, all-inorganic PSCs, Br-rich $\mathrm{CsPbIBr}_{2}$ has a bandgap of $2.05{-}2.10~\mathrm{eV}$ and superior thermal stability, making it a better choice than others, such as $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ and $\mathrm{CsPbIBr}_{2}$ [12–18]. Liang et al. [19] reported the first carbon-based, all-inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ PSC with a PCE of $8.25\%$ . Guo et al. [13] achieved an enhanced PCE from $4.73\%$ to $7.00\%$ and a high open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of $1.31{\mathrm{V}}$ for a similar $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbIBr}_{2}$ PSC by treating the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL with an $\mathrm{\SnCl_{4}}$ solution. Tan et al. [20] reported a $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ PSC with a PCE of $9.25\%$ by incorporating $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions into a $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ lattice. More recently, we found that interfacial voids can boost the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of a $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ PSC to
$1.338{\mathrm{V}} $ , coupled with a PCE of $10.20\%$ [21].
Despite this considerable progress, the PCEs achieved to date for carbon-based, all-inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbIBr}_{2}$ PSCs are still low [16,22], primarily for two reasons. One is that the wide bandgap of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ confines its light absorption to below ${\sim}600~\mathrm{nm}$ , causing a low short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ of the final PSC. The other reason is the poor electronic features of solution-processed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ films, such as a high defect density, large degree of electronic disorder, and unmatched band energy [23–25]. Modifying $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ films with a low-bandgap organic semiconductor/dye [26,27] or doping it with $\bar{\mathrm{Sn}}^{2+}$ ions to form a $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}_{1-x}\mathrm{Sn}_{x}\mathrm{IBr}_{2}$ alloy [19,28] was demonstrated to extend the film’s absorption range, which improved the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ Some other innovative strategies, such as intermolecular exchange [12], ionic incorporation [29,30], and interface modification [31,32], have also been used to regulate the growth of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ films. The improved quality of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ films effectively suppresses the non-radiative recombination of charge carriers, leading to an increased fill factor (FF) and/or $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ in the resulting PSCs. Although these attempts have been successful, the simultaneous modification of the absorption characteristics and crystal quality of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ films to improve both the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and FF/ $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ toward highly efficient PSCs has rarely been studied. Moreover, the above-mentioned strategies to extend the absorption range of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ films inevitably introduce extra organic modification layers or $\mathsf{S n}^{2+}$ ions that are prone to oxidation to ${\mathrm{Sn}}^{4+}$ in ambient air [19,26–28], which causes additional stability issues for the PSCs.
Since the first report of exchange behavior on the anion sites of halide perovskites [33,34], much attention has been paid to tailor the composition and/or microstructure of perovskite materials via halide exchange [35–39]. In principle, it is also applicable for $\mathrm{CsPbIBr}_{2}$ films to modify their composition and/or microstructure, and thus boost their absorption properties and crystal quality. However, previous studies have demonstrated that the halide exchange reaction is extremely fast in perovskite materials [33,34,36,38,40,41]. For example, Solis-Ibarra et al. [34] found that the transformation of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbBr}_{3}$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{X}_{3}$ ( $\mathrm{X=I}$ or Br) to $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}.$ - ${\mathrm{Pb}}{\mathrm{Cl}}_{3}$ is complete in $5{-}8\mathrm{min}$ . Luo et al. [40] reported that the parent $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{3}$ film changed into $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ within a few seconds during a $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Br}}_{2}$ -vapor-assisted halide-exchange process. Even for centimeter-sized perovskite single crystals, complete halide exchange can be achieved after a few days [42,43]. Rapid halide exchange not only interferes with the fine-tuning capability of the optical and crystalline properties, but also generates many structural defects in $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{IBr}_{2}$ films, leading to inferior performance of the resulting PSCs.
Herein, we propose a halide exchange strategy that offers remarkable controllability over the reaction rate to simultaneously modulate the optical and microstructural properties of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ films. This strategy involves the face-to-face pairing of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbIBr}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ films. With proper heat treatment, the halide exchange between these two films is activated and proceeds concurrently, with $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ diffusing toward the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ film and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}$ diffusing toward the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ film. In this manner, the halide exchange rate can be well-controlled by changing the heating parameters, wherein a higher temperature can accelerate the halide exchange, while a lower temperature effectively slows down or stops it. By using an optimal halide exchange temperature $(110^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and time $(2\mathrm{h})$ , a high-quality $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film can be obtained, which holds an extended absorption onset from 590 to $625~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , coarsened grains, improved crystallinity, decreased surface roughness, and reduced halide phase segregation. The film maintains these properties without sacrificing the excellent stability of the pristine $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ film. The carbon-based, all-inorganic PSC fabricated with the optimal $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film yielded a superior PCE of $10.94\%$ , together with excellent operational stability at high temperatures in ambient air.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
## Materials
CsI (ultra-dry, $99.998\%$ ), $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ (ultra-dry, $99.998\%$ ), and $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ (ultra-dry, $99.999\%$ were received from Alfa-Aesar. Dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO, anhydrous, $99.8\substack{+\%}$ ) and $N,N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF, anhydrous, $99.8\substack{+\%}\cdot$ were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ (purity, $99.8\substack{+\%}\rangle$ ) was received from Xi’an Polymer Light Technology Corp. Conductive carbon paste was bought from Shanghai MaterWin New Materials Co., Ltd., China. FTO substrates $(8~\Omega~\mathrm{{sq}^{-1})}$ were received from Yingkou OPV Tech New Energy Co., Ltd.
## Preparation of $\mathbf{CsPbIBr}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ films
The FTO substrate was ultrasonically cleaned in deionized water, acetone, and ethanol, followed by ultraviolet-ozone (UVO) treatment for $10~\mathrm{min}$ . $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ colloidal solution was then spin-coated onto the FTO substrate ( $3500~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}$ ) and annealed at $480^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $60\mathrm{min}$ in the air to form a compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\scriptstyle\left(\mathbf{-}\operatorname{TiO}_{2}\right)$ ) layer. To obtain a $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ film, the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{IBr}_{2}$ precursor solution was first prepared by dissolving $367~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{PbBr}_{2}$ and
$260~\mathrm{{mg}~C s I}$ in $1\mathrm{mL}$ DMSO. Then, the precursor was spin-coated on the $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{c}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ substrate at $1500~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}$ and $5000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $60\ s,$ after which it was heated at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{min}$ and further annealed at $280^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20~\mathrm{min}$ . The $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ film was prepared by a solvent-engineering assisted one-step spin-coating route. Specifically, $60{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ precursor obtained by mixing $160~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ , $461~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathrm{PbI}_{2},$ $78~\mathrm{mg}$ DMSO, and $600~\mathrm{{mg}}$ DMF was dripped onto the $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{c}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ substrate, and then the substrate was spun at $4500~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $20~\mathsf{s}$ . Within the first 6 s of spinning, $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ anhydrous diethyl ether was dripped continuously onto the substrate. After the annealing treatment at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ , a crystalline $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ film was obtained.
## Halide exchange treatment
First, the as-obtained $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbIBr}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ films were face-to-face paired. Then, the samples were placed on a hotplate and heated at a specific temperature, such as $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . During this stage, the halide exchange between $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ ions in $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ film and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}$ ions in $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ film proceeded slowly. By controlling the heating treatment time, the desired $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film was obtained.
## Fabrication of carbon-based, all-inorganic PSC
Commercial carbon paste was screen-printed onto the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film, which was further heated at $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $15\mathrm{min}$ in ambient air to solidify the carbon electrode. The area of the carbon electrode was confined to $0.09\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ .
The details for the characterization of $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films and carbon-based, all-inorganic PSCs can be found in our previous work [12,23].
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The key procedure for achieving a slow halide exchange is shown schematically in Fig. 1a, wherein the $\mathrm{CsPbIBr}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ films were face-to-face paired. With the proper heating treatment, the $\bar{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}}$ ions in the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ film tended to vaporize and penetrate into the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{IBr}_{2}$ film, during which the $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ ions in $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{IBr}_{2}$ film concurrently diffused into the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ film [39]. Thus, the halide exchange of $\bar{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}}$ and $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ ions occurs between $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ films, producing $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3-x}\mathrm{Br}_{x}$ films, respectively. In this case, the halide exchange rate is dominated by the release rate of $\bar{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}}$ ions in the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ film. Therefore, it can be well-controlled by the heating recipe, in which a higher temperature accelerates the halide exchange, but a lower temperature will effectively slow down or even stop it, as described below.
Fig. 1b presents the photographs of a series of samples prepared under different halide exchange temperatures, including room temperature (RT), 90, 110, 130, 150, and $170^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ respectively. The colors of the films formed at RT and $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ showed negligible changes after halide exchange, while the colors tended to deepen for the others upon increasing the halide exchange temperature. The color changes of the samples were well-supported by their ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis) absorption spectra in Fig. 1c. The films prepared at RT and $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ yielded a similar absorption onset of $590\mathrm{nm}$ , which corresponds to a bandgap of $2.10\mathrm{eV}$ and is in line with that of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ films reported previously [14,31]. By comparison, the absorption onsets shifted to 603, 615, 637, and $655\mathrm{nm}$ for the samples prepared at 110, 130, 150, and $170^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{:}}$ respectively. The greater red-shift in the absorption onset was generally accompanied by a greater portion of $\bar{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}}$ in the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film [44]. Therefore, the above results indicate that when the heating temperature reaches $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the halide exchange is activated, and a higher temperature increases the exchange rate. Since a slower halide exchange rate is expected to form a $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film with fewer defects, we chose $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as the optimized halide exchange temperature in subsequent experiments. Indeed, as exhibited in Fig. S1, the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film prepared at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ exhibited a more intense photoluminescence (PL) peak than the one obtained at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ indicating fewer non-recombination sites and, thus, structural defects in the former. Hereinafter, the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film with a halide exchange time of $^{0\mathrm{h}}$ represents the parent $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ film, which is adopted as a control sample.
Fig. 1d gives the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films prepared at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with different halide exchange times of 0, 1, 2, and $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively. The clear diffraction peaks of the (001)/(100), (110), and (002)/(200) crystal planes for cubic perovskite $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ were observed in all samples, while no impurity phase was identified [37]. This indicates that halide exchange has little impact on the crystal phase of the as-investigated films. Upon prolonging the halide exchange time, the dominant diffraction peaks, such as those of the (100), (110), and (200) planes, progressively shifted towards low diffraction angles, which was ascribed to the increased $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}$ portion in $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films [36,37]. Meanwhile, the intensity of the dominant XRD peak increased following the same trend. We ascribe this phenomenon to the improved crystallinity of the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films upon increasing the halide exchange time. That is to say, a slow halide exchange reaction repaired crystal defects in the parent $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ film. The UV-vis absorption spectra in Fig. 1e show that the absorption onsets are 590, 607, 625, and $636\mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films with halide exchange durations of 0, 1, 2, and $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively. These results indicate a higher $\bar{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}}$ portion in the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films prepared with a longer halide exchange time, in good accordance with the XRD results.
The morphology characteristics of the as-prepared $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films were studied by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and atomic force microscopy (AFM), respectively. The SEM images in Fig. $2\mathsf{a}_{1}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{d}_{1}$ show that all $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films have a full surface coverage to ensure effective light absorption and prevent the possible formation of shuttling shutting paths in the final carbonbased, all-inorganic PSCs. Meanwhile, the average grain sizes were estimated to be 0.64, 0.68, 0.79, and $0.90\upmu\mathrm{m}$ for the films obtained with halide exchange durations of 0, 1, 2, and $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively (Fig. S2). The increase in the average grain size with the halide exchange time can be assigned to the organic $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ ions from the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ film. These ions can promote the grain coarsening in the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film via the Ostwald ripening mechanism, as proposed in previous studies [45,46]. The AFM images in Fig. $2\mathbf{a}_{\mathrm{1}}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{d}_{\mathrm{1}}$ support the SEM results. The root-mean-square (RMS) roughness values were evaluated to be 22.3, 19.4, 18.7, and $15.5\mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films with halide exchange durations of 0, 1, 2, and $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively. This indicates that the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film surface became flatter upon prolonging the halide exchange time. Taken together, all of the above results indicate that a slow halide exchange reaction rate produces pure-phase $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films with a narrower bandgap, improved crystallinity, enlarged grains, and reduced surface roughness. Moreover, such characteristics become increasingly better upon extending the halide exchange time.
Further, the influence of halide exchange on the photophysical properties and composition of $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films were investigated by taking films with halide exchange durations of 0 and $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ as examples. Fig. 3a presents the steady-state PL spectra of the films grown on insulated glass substrates. The film with a halide exchange duration of $^{0\mathrm{h}}$ , namely the parent $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ film, exhibited a PL peak at ${\sim}574~\mathrm{nm}$ , consistent with those of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbIBr}_{2}$ materials reported previously [12,14,32]. By comparison, the PL peak of the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film with a halide exchange duration of $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ underwent a red-shift to $611~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , indicating its narrower bandgap. Its PL intensity is much stronger than that of the parent film. Since both films were deposited on insulated glass substrates, a more intense PL peak indicates weaker non-radiative recombination of charge carriers in the related $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film. The time-resolved RL (TRPL) spectra in Fig. 3b show that the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film with a halide exchange time of $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ exhibits slower PL decay, in line with the steady-state PL results [47]. In particular, the average PL lifetime $(\tau_{\mathrm{ave}})$ values were fitted to be 10.8 and 20.9 ns for the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films with halide exchange times of 0 and $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively.
In terms of Br-rich, mixed-halide inorganic perovskites, the $\mathrm{\Pb-Br}$ bond is stronger than the $\mathrm{\mathbf{P}b\mathrm{-}I}$ bond and, thus, can spontaneously drive $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}$ out to generate a Br-rich domain phase. Such a process easily forms small-bandgap Irich domains and large-bandgap Br-rich domains, causing undesired halide phase segregation [48]. To gain insight into such a phenomenon in the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films, their steady-state PL spectra after being illuminated with a $478–\mathrm{nm}$ laser $(30\mathrm{\mW\cm}^{-2})$ for different durations were recorded. Both films were deposited on $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{c}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ substrates to coincide with carbon-based, all-inorganic PSCs. As shown in Fig. 3c, the PL peak underwent a clear red-shift, and its intensity increased with the illumination time for the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film with a halide exchange time of $^{0\mathrm{h}}$ . These results indicate unavoidable halide phase segregation, as frequently observed in previous $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ films [12,28,48,49]. By contrast, the changes in the PL peak during continuous illumination were greatly reduced for the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film with a halide exchange time of $2\mathrm{h}$ , as exhibited in Fig. 3d. Namely, the halide exchange strategy with a slow reaction rate can effectively suppress undesired halide phase segregation in $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ films.
Fig. 4a provides the core-level N 1s X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra of $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films with halide exchange durations of 0 and $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . Similar to the parent sample, no $\mathrm{~N~}$ atoms were detected in the film after halide exchange. That is to say, despite the exchange of $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ and $\bar{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}}$ between the parent $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ films, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ cations were not present in the final $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film. Fig. 4b–e provide the corresponding core-level Cs 3d, Pb 4f, I 3d, and Br 3d XPS spectra. Their binding energies are similar to those of $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films reported earlier [12,23]. The slight differences in the binding energies between $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films with halide exchange durations of 0 and $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ were mainly related to their different compositions [12,23,50]. In addition, from the core-level I 3d, and Br 3d XPS spectra, the relative atomic ratios of $[\mathrm{I}]/[\mathrm{Br}]$ were estimated to 0.46 and 1.35 for the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films with halide exchange durations of 0 and $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively, verifying again the increased portion of $\bar{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}^{-}}$ ions in the film after halide exchange.
Next, we studied the effects of slow halide exchange on the performance of carbon-based, all-inorganic PSCs with a layer stacking of $\mathrm{FTO/c\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}/C s P b I_{1+x}B r_{2-x}/}$ carbon. Fig. 5a gives the cross-sectional SEM image of a typical PSC, with each of the functional layers clearly visible. The thickness of the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film was approximately ${\sim}400~\mathrm{nm}$ . To obtain a reliable conclusion, 20 independent PSCs based on the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films with halide exchange durations of 0, 1, 2, and $^{3\mathrm{{h}}}$ were fabricated from four batches. Fig. S3a presents the statistical PCEs of all the as-fabricated $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs. The average PCEs were evaluated to be $7.67\%$ , $8.36\%$ . $9.86\%$ , and $6.82\%$ , respectively. These results make it clear that the halide exchange strategy with a slow reaction rate and suitably long reaction time is an effective method to produce carbon-based, all-inorganic PSCs with improved performance, particularly at the optimal halide exchange time of $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . Furthermore, the corresponding statistical $J_{s c},$ FF, and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ results in Fig. S3b–d indicate that the improved PCEs mainly come from the enhanced $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and FF values.
The current density versus voltage $(J{-}V)$ curves of the best-performing carbon-based, all-inorganic $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs with halide exchange durations of 0, 1, 2, and $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ are provided in Fig. 5b. The PSC based on the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film with a halide exchange time of $^{0\mathrm{~h~}}$ —the parent $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ film—shows a PCE of $8.21\%$ , together with a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $10.43~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}$ , an FF of 0.64, and a $V_{o c}$ of $1.230\mathrm{V}$ . By comparison, the PSCs with halide exchange times of 1 and $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ exhibit much improved performances. In particular, the PCE of the latter improved to $10.94\%$ , for which its $J_{s c},\quad V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ and FF were $12.30\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , $1.186\mathrm{V}$ , and 0.75, respectively. However, when the halide exchange time was extended to $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , the PCE of the resultant $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSC decreased to $7.79\%$ . Further comparisons revealed that such an inferior PCE was mainly related to the low $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF. We attribute this phenomenon to the degraded stability of the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film prepared with a halide exchange time of $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . As shown in Fig. S4, the film was partially decomposed after the deposition of the carbon electrode in ambient air. Therefore, it appears that the optimal halide exchange time is around $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , which ensures the superior performance of $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs.
The external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of the best-performing $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs were also measured, as given in Fig. 5c. The photocurrent onsets were determined to be ${\sim}590$ , 607, 625, and $636~\mathrm{{nm}}$ for those with halide exchange durations of 0, 1, 2, and $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ respectively, which are consistent with the bandgaps of the corresponding $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films. Furthermore, as provided in Fig. S5, the integrated $J_{s c}$ of the EQE spectra are 8.42,
9.81, 11.29, and $10.64\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs obtained using films with halide exchange durations of $0,1,2$ , and $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively. They are in accordance with those derived from the light $J{-}V$ curves. The slight discrepancy mainly resulted from the spectral mismatch between the EQE light source and the solar simulator [15,29,45,48]. In addition, one can see that the EQEs are higher for the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs prepared from films with halide exchange times of 1 and $^{2\mathrm{h},}$ revealing their superior photoelectric conversion.
The stability characteristics of the typical champion $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs were also studied. Fig. 5d gives the stabilized current density outputs near the maximum power points of the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs with halide exchange times of 0 and $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively. It is clear that during the test period, both $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs showed negligible photocurrent degradation in ambient air with continuous $\mathrm{AM}\ 1.5\mathrm{G}$ illumination, suggesting their superior operational stability. Stable PCEs can also be derived from Fig. 5d, which are $7.73\%$ and $9.96\%$ for the PSCs with halide exchange times of 0 and $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively. These values are slightly smaller than those obtained from the $J{-}V$ curves in Fig. 5b, manifesting that general $J{-}V$ hysteresis also occurs in our carbon-based, all-inorganic $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs [31,48,49].
Additionally, the thermal stability of the PSCs was evaluated in a glovebox atmosphere at $85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $15\mathrm{h}$ . As seen from Fig. 5e, the performance degradation of the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs with halide exchange times of 0 and $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ did not exceed $6\%$ , revealing their similar thermal stability. In addition, the long-term storage stability of the as-obtained $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs was monitored in ambient air with a controlled temperature of $25\pm3^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and relative humidity (RH) of $40\%~\pm~10\%$ . The results in Fig. 5f indicate that the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSC fabricated with a halide exchange time of $0\mathrm{{h}}$ retained $85\%$ of its initial PCE after being tested for $45\mathrm{d}$ . As for the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSC obtained with a halide exchange time of $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , its performance degradation was below $20\%$ , which is comparable to that of the former. The above stability investigations make clear that, despite an increased $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{~I~}^{-}}$ portion due to slow halide exchange, the stability of the resulting $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs is still satisfactory. In particular, the one fabricated with a halide exchange time of $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ displayed similar stability features as the PSC based on the parent $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ film.
Overall, the halide exchange strategy with a slow reaction rate can transform pristine $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ films into $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ $(0~<~x~<~2)$ films that possess narrower bandgaps, improved crystallinity, enlarged grains, reduced surface roughness, and suppressed halide phase segregation. Moreover, by optimizing the halide exchange parameters, a carbon-based, all-inorganic PSC with a much-improved PCE and similarly superior stability can be obtained. The modified PCE has an improved $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ due to the narrower bandgap of the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film after the halide exchange treatment. The enhanced crystalline quality of the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films may help reduce the non-radiative recombination of charge carriers, which can ultimately result in an enlarged FF and/or $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ [23– 25,51]. To validate this speculation, the kinetic characteristics of charge carriers in typical $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}\mathrm{~F~}$ SCs with halide exchange times of 0 and $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ were studied by photocurrent/photovoltage (TPC/TPV) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), respectively. Fig. 6a, b provide the TPC and TPV curves of the typical carbon-based, all-inorganic $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs with halide exchange times of 0 and $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively. The PSC with a halide exchange time of $2\mathrm{h}$ has a faster TPC decay and a slower TPV decay than the one with a halide exchange time of $^{0\mathrm{h}}$ . For example, its TPC and TPV average lifetime $(\tau_{\mathrm{ave}})$ values were fitted to be $1.54\upmu\mathrm{s}$ and $1.06\mathrm{ms}$ , respectively, while the corresponding values were $2.81~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ and $0.47~\mathrm{ms}$ for the other. These results reveal the more effective interfacial extraction and reduced non-radiative recombination of charge carriers in the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSC with a halide exchange time of $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ [12,23,45,50]. Fig. 6c presents the Nyquist plots of the asinvestigated $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSCs, in which the clear arcs are related to the interfacial recombination of charge carries, labeled by the recombination resistance $(R_{\mathrm{rec}})$ . The $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSC with a halide exchange time of $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ has a much larger $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ indicating its weaker non-radiative recombination [12,15,23,45,50]. The TPC/TPV and EIS results support this speculation.
## CONCLUSIONS
The halide exchange happens between the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ films physically paired together, which proceeds with a slow reaction rate that could be wellcontrolled by changing the heat treatment temperature. It thus offers the ability to simultaneously finely modulate the optical and microstructural characteristics of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ films. By optimizing the halide exchange temperature and time, the parent $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ film could be transformed into high-quality $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ films that exhibited an extended absorption onset from 590 to $625~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , coarsened grains, improved crystallinity, suppressed halide phase separation, but without degradation of the thermostability and moisture resistance, in contrast to the parent film. The carbon-based, all-inorganic PSC based on the optimized $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ film (halide exchange temperature and time of $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ ) showed a PCE of $10.94\%$ , which is much higher than the bestperforming one fabricated with the parent $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ film that yielded a PCE of $8.21\%$ . The $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{1+x}\mathrm{Br}_{2-x}$ PSC possessed comparable stability to the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{IBr}}_{2}$ film-based PSC, and it retained up to $96\%$ or $80\%$ of its initial PCE after being tested at $85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $15\mathrm{h}$ in a glovebox at $25\pm3^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $40\%\pm10\%$ RH for 45 days in ambient air.
